The phylogenetic relationships of BtCN-Virome with their reference sequences have been reconstructed based on 
the full-length RdRp sequences at the order or family levels. We prepared the RdRp full-length sequence set of
BtCN-Virome, ZOVER-bat, and EVRD-aa by separately sampling the representatives of their vcAAI90 clusters. We 
then conducted all-versus-all alignment using blastp to find sequences with an alignment length of ≥ 200 (i.e.,
at least half of the average length of RdRp). We retrieved from the blast file all full-length RdRp sequences
of BtCN-Virome and ZOVER-bat that had an alignment length of ≥ 200 against EVRD-aa sequences from  the same 
order or family, which allowed us to recruit as many BtCN-Virome sequences in the phylogenetic analysis as 
possible. To minimize the data size of an order or family, we did not include all full-length RdRp sequences 
of EVRD-aa within the order or family but only sampled those showing the highest bit scores against the above
sequences of BtCN-Virome and ZOVER-bat. This also included divergent sequences that are not members of the 
order or family and sequences that were aligned against a different region of the RdRp core, which would 
disturb the robustness of phylogenetic analysis. Therefore, we conducted an initial alignment using E-INS-i, 
available in mafft version 7.470 and trimmed using trimAL version 1.4 to remove columns containing ≥ 67% of 
gap. A sequence containing ≥ 20% of gap and/or X (i.e., undetermined residue) was also removed from the 
alignment. Then a maximum-likelihood tree was inferred using IQ-TREE version 1.6.12 with 1,000 ultrafast 
bootstrap replicates and the best-fit model that was automatically determined by the ModelFinder module. The
initial tree was manually checked to identify the remote neighbors (i.e., unrelated taxa) of the order or 
family. All sequences within these neighbors were removed from the initial alignment, but a few sequences of
the order- or family-level neighbor within the same class were retained to determine the phylogenetic root of
the order or family. After this refinement, the remaining sequences were then subjected to another round of 
phylogenetic reconstruction as described above. The final phylogenetic tree can be visualized using FigTree 
version 1.4.3.

################################################################################################################

Each tar ball contains 13 files.
xxx.tar.gz
	|---xxx.faa (n = 1):					Original sequence set used for inital phylogenetic reconstruction.
	|---xxx.mafft.faa (n = 1):				Multiple sequence alignment of original sequences using mafft.
	|---xxx.mafft.trimmed.faa (n = 1):		Multiple sequence alignment after removing columns containting ≥ 67%
	|										of gap and removing sequences containing ≥ 20% of gap and/or X, used 
	|										for the inital phylogenetic reconstruction.
	|---xxx.mafft.trimmed.80.faa (n = 1):		Multiple sequence alignment after removing unrelated sequences, used 
	|										for the final phylogenetic reconstruction.
	|---xxx.mafft.trimmed.80.faa.xxx (n = 9):	Tree files based on the xxx.mafft.trimmed.80.faa generated by IQ-TREE.
	|---xxx.mafft.trimmed.80.mc.faa.xxx (n = 9):	Tree files based on the manually-checked xxx.mafft.trimmed.80.faa
													generated by IQ-TREE.
	
